    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense, i guess. so is it like they just sit on the money until everything is perfect? do they have any say in the deal or are they just like a vault for the cash and the paperwork? i'm just worried about something going wrong while the money is just sitting there with a stranger.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The escrow agent acts more like a secure vault than an active participant, holding funds and titles without having any say in the terms of your deal or the ability to force the transaction to close. Their primary role is administrative: they simply release the money only when all agreed-upon conditions, such as the final financing and clear title, are officially verified by their inspections. If something were to go wrong during the process, they are generally prohibited from canceling the deal unless there is a major breach of contract they identified, ensuring you don't lose your earnest money unfairly. This standardized protection is why the system is trusted even though a stranger temporarily controls your significant funds.",
